mike: Quick Q — running into flaky tests around cancellation while streaming. Node SDK tests sometimes hang and the server still sees the connection open. Anyone run into signal leaks on long-lived streams?

elaine: Yep. We've seen this when AbortController is used only to close readable stream but not the underlying socket in older node fetch implementations. Which runtime are you on?

mike: Node 18.17.0 (native fetch). The client calls AbortController.abort() after 30s timeout, but the server logs show no FIN and the stream continues for a bit.

jun: Are you using stream piping to a Response.body reader? In our code we had to explicitly call reader.cancel() and also close any TransformStreams. Excerpt we used:```js
const ac = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 30000);
const res = await fetch(url, { signal: ac.signal });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
try {
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    // process chunk
  }
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
    // explicit cancel helps ensure underlying socket closes
    try { await reader.cancel(); } catch(e) {}
  }
}
```

ryan: For Go we rely on context.WithTimeout — the runtime cancels the request and the underlying TCP is closed, but only if you don't re-use the same http.Client with weird transport settings. Also note that if a server supports HTTP/2 and tries to stream more frames, it may keep the connection alive until it sees RST_STREAM.

mike: Interesting — our Python tests using requests+sseclient behave differently: after canceling the thread we sometimes get a partial chunk and the generator yields one more item. Is that expected?

elaine: Python's requests isn't async and streaming yields are buffered by urllib3. You need to close the response to force a socket close:```py
resp = requests.get(url, stream=True, timeout=30)
try:
    for chunk in resp.iter_content(chunk_size=1024):
        if should_cancel():
            resp.close()  # important
            break
finally:
    resp.close()
```

jun: Another cross-cutting issue: retries. If your client retry logic triggers on network errors, make sure abort isn’t treated as transient. Otherwise you can have a retry loop that spawns new streams after abort, leaving server-side work duplicated. We add a cancellation flag to disable retries when AbortError/context canceled.

mike: Good call. In Node we were using a generic retry wrapper that caught any Error. Adjusted to:```js
if (err.name === 'AbortError' || signal.aborted) throw err; // don't retry on explicit abort
// else retry
```

ryan: For the Rust SDK we use tokio_util::sync::CancellationToken. One subtlety: dropping the token's receiver doesn't necessarily close the hyper connection instantly — you need to call cancel_token.cancel() early and then make sure any pending read loops check cancellation between awaits.

elaine: Also watch for long keep-alive settings on load balancers. We had cases where the LB held the TCP open even after the client aborted, making it look like the server kept streaming. Lowering idle_timeout helped.

